    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Most comms rooms get checked the day something breaks. By then the UPS batteries have been dead for two years. A short, regular walk catches slow failures while they are cheap to fix. Here is the checklist, and how to record it without a laptop on the rack.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A comms room check is a physical walk: power, temperature, cables, access and tidiness.</li><li>The usual killers: dead UPS batteries, blocked cooling and stored combustibles.</li><li>Record each item at the rack, with a photo and a reading.</li><li>Photos with times become proof for insurers and audits.</li><li>Quickler records the physical check. It is not a network or logical audit tool.</li><li>The check is only as good as the person doing it.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Power",
      "heading": "UPS, feeds and battery life",
      "html": "<p>Start with power. Is the UPS online, not on bypass? Note the load and the battery age. Sealed lead-acid batteries usually last three to five years. Check the last self-test and whether it passed. Are breakers labelled and none tripped? Is the rack fed properly, not by an extension lead under the door?</p><p>A UPS with tired batteries looks healthy until the first real cut. Then it drops the load in seconds. Photograph the display.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Environment",
      "heading": "Temperature, cooling and airflow",
      "html": "<p>Heat is the second killer. Read the temperature against what the kit expects. Often the low twenties Celsius for a small room. Is the aircon or fan running and not iced up? Are filters clean and vents clear? Feel for hot spots behind the rack.</p><p>A blocked filter does not announce itself. The temperature just creeps. A monthly reading with a photo makes the drift visible.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Cables and tidiness",
      "heading": "Tidy, labelled, nothing stored inside",
      "html": "<p>Tidy, labelled leads are not vanity. A tangle hides faults and blocks airflow. Note untidy runs and unlabelled cables. Is the floor clear? Is anything combustible stored inside? Any sign of water or dust?</p><p>Finish with access. Does the door lock? Is the access list current? Is there a visitor log? An open comms room is a security Fault, and ISO 27001 covers it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
